    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-131387",
      "knowledge_point_id": "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<2.01).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If $Z∼N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<2.01)=P(Z\\le 2.01)=Φ(2.01).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/300726cc3cff6cb943d627693bdea3da57ca7bb814988b55e9e3885a03faef42.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $Φ(2.01)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $2.0$ and the column that corresponds to $0.01.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le 2.01)=Φ(2.01)=0.9778.\n$$",
