  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404",
    "id": "q-131399",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-131399",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge -2.15).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ | $\\,0.0154\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ | $\\,0.0197\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge -2.15).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/f814b532742940206d119c1aa30746b128ab6ede249128e55fc949a1051220c9.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.15) & =1-P(Z<-2.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/9e2cf363bd0732c4669c4653d83f505826af605f2bc719a4f39135d233456b57.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.15)=0.0158.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.15) & =1-Φ(-2.15) \\\\  & =1-0.0158 \\\\  & =0.9842.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
